1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,880
This is Doing and Feeling, Episode 8, The Case for Illusion. Right now, without moving your eyes, ask yourself how much of what you see is in color. For almost everyone, the answer comes back instantly, all of it.

2
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:21,040
In 2020, the psychologists Michael Cohen, Thomas Botch, and Caroline Robertson tested that confidence.

3
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:26,780
Participants wore virtual reality headsets with eye tracking and explored immersive scenes.

4
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:30,860
A historic site, a street dance, an orchestra rehearsal.

5
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:35,980
The software kept color only where each person was looking and drained it from everything else,

6
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:38,080
turning the periphery black and white.

7
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:40,360
Most participants never noticed.

8
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:45,560
In the most extreme condition, with color left in only a small central patch,

9
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:50,680
less than 5% of the visual field, nearly a third of them still did not notice.

10
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:55,420
That's a fact about peripheral vision. Nobody thinks the eye is perfect.

11
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:59,840
The eye is not what failed, though. What failed was the report.

12
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:03,720
People were asked whether anything had changed in what they saw,

13
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:08,700
and most reported nothing amiss, although much of their visual field had lost its color.

14
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:15,280
read that. The realist reading says the brain filled in the color, so the experience really

15
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:20,900
was colored, and introspection got the experience right while the world was different. The other

16
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:25,220
reading says people's judgments about their experience ran ahead of anything going on.

17
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:31,760
Either way, the sense of a rich, uniform, colorful visual field turns out to be a construction,

18
00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:37,180
and introspection did not flag it as one. Today's question is how far that goes.

19
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:41,260
Could introspection be wrong not just about the details of experience,

20
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:46,640
but about its most basic character, the very thing that makes the hard problem seem hard?

21
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:51,960
That is the claim of illusionism, and today it gets the strongest hearing I can give it,

22
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:54,180
followed by its hardest objections.

23
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:59,700
Start with what the view actually says, because it's routinely caricatured.

24
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:03,640
The modern statement is Keith Frankish's 2016 paper,

25
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:09,980
illusionism as a theory of consciousness published as a target article in the journal of consciousness

26
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:16,380
studies with a symposium of replies later collected in a book frankish draws a line

27
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:21,740
between two things that ordinary talk runs together on one side is consciousness in the

28
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:27,560
everyday functional sense you're awake you see the tomato you can attend to it think about it

29
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:33,600
report it. When you stub your toe, a state arises that registers damage, seizes your attention,

30
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:39,740
and makes you want it to stop. Illusionists deny none of this. On the other side is phenomenal

31
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:45,800
consciousness in the philosopher's sense. Special qualities of experience, qualia, that are supposed

32
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:52,500
to be intrinsic, private, ineffable, and known directly. The redness of the red, considered as

33
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:58,800
something over and above everything the experience does. That is what illusionism denies. Frankish's

34
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:03,580
claim is that there are no such phenomenal properties. What exist are what he calls

35
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:09,640
quasi-phenomenal properties. Physical properties of brain states that introspection represents as

36
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:15,720
having that special phenomenal character. The representation is real. The character it attributes

37
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:21,420
is not. So what am I supposed to say about a toothache, that it only seems to hurt?

38
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:27,520
It hurts. Everything that hurting does is real. The alarm, the distraction, the misery,

39
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:33,600
the plans to see a dentist. What the illusionist denies is a further private glow that floats free

40
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:38,280
of all that, which introspection seems to point at when you attend to the pain in a philosophical

41
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:45,800
mood. The hurting is real. The extra, ineffable quality is the illusion. Frankish also distinguishes

42
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:52,060
strong illusionism from weak. Weak illusionism says phenomenal properties exist but are not quite

43
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:58,060
as they seem. He argues that position is unstable because once you strip away the strange features,

44
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:03,840
what's left is functional and representational anyway. So he defends the strong version.

45
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:09,140
And he insists on a positive research program, which he calls the illusion problem.

46
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:14,040
Explain why introspection represents our states as having phenomenal properties

47
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:17,020
and why that representation is so compelling.

48
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:19,400
Compelling is the key word.

49
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:24,320
In the Müller-Lyer illusion, two equal lines with arrowheads pointing in or out

50
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:26,840
look unequal even after you've measured them.

51
00:04:27,340 --> 00:04:30,220
Knowing the explanation does not switch an illusion off.

52
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:35,880
So if illusionism is true, your sense of an inner glow should survive your accepting the theory,

53
00:04:36,060 --> 00:04:40,680
and its stubborn obviousness is not, by itself, evidence against the view.

54
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:48,920
The view's deepest roots are in Daniel Dennett, who died in 2024 and who spent half a century on this project.

55
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:56,580
In a 1988 paper, Quining Qualia, Dennett went after the very concept of qualia as philosophers define it.

56
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:58,900
His method was the intuition pump,

57
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:02,840
a carefully designed story that shows you how your intuitions work.

58
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:07,640
The best known involves two professional coffee tasters at the same company.

59
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:12,220
Both have tasted the house blend for years, and both have stopped liking it.

60
00:05:12,440 --> 00:05:16,500
One says the coffee tastes the same to him, but his standards have changed.

61
00:05:16,860 --> 00:05:21,800
The other says his taste buds must have changed, so the coffee now tastes different to him.

62
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:26,160
Dennett pressed the question, what could possibly settle which of them is right?

63
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:34,780
Perhaps there is no fact of the matter at all, and if a concept generates questions with no possible answers, maybe the concept is confused.

64
00:05:35,140 --> 00:05:40,700
His 1991 book, Consciousness Explained, attacked a picture he called the Cartesian Theater.

65
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:50,380
The idea that somewhere in the brain there's a place where it all comes together, a screen watched by an inner audience, and that a content becomes conscious at the moment it arrives there.

66
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:54,440
The brain, he argued, has no such place and no such moment.

67
00:05:54,720 --> 00:06:00,180
What it has are multiple drafts, parallel streams of content, constantly edited and revised.

68
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:02,700
His favorite example was ColorFi.

69
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:11,560
In 1976, Paul Kohlers and Michael Fon Grunow flashed a red dot and then quickly, a green dot a short distance away.

70
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:18,100
People see one dot moving and turning green partway along its path before the green dot has even appeared.

71
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:19,860
So how did the brain know?

72
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:25,900
Either it held the experience back and edited it before release, which Dennett called the Stalin-esque story,

73
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:30,460
or you saw it correctly and your memory was rewritten, the Orwellian story.

74
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:35,020
Dennett's provocative conclusion was that there is no fact about which is true

75
00:06:35,020 --> 00:06:39,440
because there is no finish line in the brain where experience officially happens.

76
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:41,760
No finish line, no theater.

77
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:47,180
A distributed system has no global clock either, and things still happen in it.

78
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:50,120
They do, and Dennett would take the analogy gladly.

79
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:57,580
Ask when a write really happened, apart from what the logs say and what the other nodes later see, and there may be no answer.

80
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:00,360
His claim is that the brain is like that.

81
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:02,360
Things happen in it all the time.

82
00:07:02,700 --> 00:07:07,040
What's missing is a single moment when one of them officially becomes experience.

83
00:07:07,660 --> 00:07:10,660
In place of the theater, Dennett offered a method and a slogan.

84
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:13,220
The method was heterophenomenology.

85
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:19,440
Take people's reports about their experience with total seriousness, as data about how things seem to them,

86
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:25,980
the reports are accurate descriptions of what's happening inside. The slogan was fame in the brain.

87
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:31,660
A content becomes conscious the way a person becomes famous, by winning influence over the

88
00:07:31,660 --> 00:07:36,840
rest of the system, not by entering a special room. And he borrowed an image from computing,

89
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:43,360
the user illusion. Your laptop shows you folders and a trash can. There are no tiny folders on the

90
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:48,860
chip, but the interface is real, useful, and the only way you ever deal with what's underneath.

91
00:07:50,380 --> 00:07:56,780
the felt inner world is the brain's interface to itself. Illusionism needs evidence that

92
00:07:56,780 --> 00:08:03,560
introspection can be wrong about our own minds. There's a lot of it. In 1977, Richard Nisbet and

93
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:09,400
Timothy Wilson published a classic paper with the title telling more than we can know. In one study,

94
00:08:09,780 --> 00:08:14,900
shoppers choosing among identical items laid out in a row tended to pick the one on the right.

95
00:08:15,660 --> 00:08:19,720
Asked why, they cited quality and denied that position had mattered.

96
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:24,640
People confidently report the causes of their own choices and are often wrong.

97
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:31,220
The philosopher Eric S. H. Witzgabel has argued that the problem goes deeper, to experience itself.

98
00:08:31,580 --> 00:08:36,020
In a 2008 paper on the unreliability of naive introspection,

99
00:08:36,340 --> 00:08:41,300
he collected basic questions about current experience on which careful people disagree.

100
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:46,760
How clear the periphery of the visual field is, whether emotions have a bodily feel,

101
00:08:47,020 --> 00:08:48,820
whether thought has a sensory character.

102
00:08:49,060 --> 00:08:53,800
These are questions about what experience is like right now, and introspection gives

103
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:54,820
conflicting answers.

104
00:08:55,220 --> 00:09:00,820
The psychologist Russell Hurlburt has spent decades on a method called descriptive experience

105
00:09:00,820 --> 00:09:01,340
sampling.

106
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:07,080
A beeper goes off at random moments, and you describe exactly what was in your experience

107
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:08,080
just before it.

108
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:15,220
In one study of 30 people, inner speech showed up in about a quarter of samples on average, but with enormous variation.

109
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:19,580
Some people reported none at all, others three quarters of the time.

110
00:09:20,380 --> 00:09:30,160
Hurlburt's long-running conclusion is that people's armchair generalizations about their own inner lives are far less trustworthy than careful reports of specific moments.

111
00:09:30,160 --> 00:09:38,500
And in 2015, the neurologist Adam Zemmen gave a name to a fantasia, the lifelong absence of voluntary visual imagery.

112
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:44,360
Some people discover only as adults that when others say they can picture a face, they mean it literally.

113
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:51,900
A difference as large as having inner pictures or not can go unnoticed for decades because everyone uses the same words.

114
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:55,700
In episode 10, we'll add choice blindness to this file.

115
00:09:55,980 --> 00:09:59,960
All of that shows introspection is unreliable about detail.

116
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:04,140
It does not show it's wrong about there being experience at all.

117
00:10:04,340 --> 00:10:08,480
You're sliding from sometimes mistaken to mistaken about everything.

118
00:10:08,820 --> 00:10:13,140
That's exactly the right objection, and the illusionist has to answer it.

119
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:16,800
Local unreliability does not establish global error.

120
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:19,560
What illusionists add is a second step.

121
00:10:19,900 --> 00:10:24,660
They argue that we can explain why we'd believe in phenomenal properties even if there were none,

122
00:10:24,660 --> 00:10:31,820
and that if such an explanation works, believing in them anyway is like believing in a ghost after you've found the projector.

123
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:33,980
Which brings us to the metaproblem.

124
00:10:35,200 --> 00:10:38,300
Last episode ended with Chalmers' metaproblem.

125
00:10:38,440 --> 00:10:40,460
Why do we think there is a hard problem?

126
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:46,920
His 2018 paper, which drew a large set of replies, calls the culprit's problem intuitions.

127
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:48,720
He sorts them into families.

128
00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:50,760
Explanatory intuitions.

129
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,280
Experience cannot be explained physically.

130
00:10:54,660 --> 00:11:00,900
intuitions, experience is not physical. Knowledge intuitions, like Mary's, modal intuitions,

131
00:11:01,080 --> 00:11:06,900
like zombies, and these intuitions run deep. The developmental psychologist Paul Bloom has

132
00:11:06,900 --> 00:11:12,240
argued that children are natural dualists, readily treating minds as separable from bodies.

133
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:17,980
What might the answer look like? The answers I find most promising involve introspective models.

134
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:24,060
The brain represents its own perceptual states in a simplified way, as primitive qualities with

135
00:11:24,660 --> 00:11:30,600
structure because it has no need to represent the underlying machinery. A system with a model like

136
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:36,460
that would find its own states ineffable, intrinsic, and impossible to derive from anything physical

137
00:11:36,460 --> 00:11:42,300
because the model offers nothing to derive them from. Then comes the twist that makes the metaproblem

138
00:11:42,300 --> 00:11:48,740
dangerous for realists. Suppose we find a complete physical explanation of why we say there's a hard

139
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:54,620
problem, and that explanation never needs to mention experience itself. Then our beliefs

140
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:59,340
about experience would be explained without experience playing any role. That threatens

141
00:11:59,340 --> 00:12:04,480
to debunk them, the way learning that a belief was produced by a process indifferent to its truth

142
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:10,280
undermines the belief. Chalmers takes this seriously. Either the illusionist is right,

143
00:12:10,440 --> 00:12:15,700
or the realist must show that experience itself plays some role in producing our talk about it.

144
00:12:16,300 --> 00:12:21,800
That's why he treats illusionism as a serious rival to his own view, not as a fringe position.

145
00:12:22,260 --> 00:12:23,660
Here's what bugs me.

146
00:12:23,860 --> 00:12:28,560
Any language model trained on human text will reproduce problem intuitions.

147
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:35,760
Ask one about its experience, and it may tell you its inner life is mysterious and hard to put into words.

148
00:12:36,100 --> 00:12:39,120
Does that make the metaproblem easier or harder?

149
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:40,740
Both, I think.

150
00:12:41,260 --> 00:12:46,120
Easier, because it proves that problem intuition behavior can be produced by a mechanism we can

151
00:12:46,120 --> 00:12:46,600
inspect.

152
00:12:47,100 --> 00:12:51,900
Harder, because it shows the behavior can arise for reasons that have nothing to do with having

153
00:12:51,900 --> 00:12:57,440
experience or even with having an introspective model, simply by learning from people who talk

154
00:12:57,440 --> 00:12:57,880
this way.

155
00:12:58,120 --> 00:13:01,740
In a language model, the reports alone tell you very little.

156
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:06,220
That turns out to be one of the central obstacles in episodes 12 and 13.

157
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:11,400
Several researchers have tried to say what the mechanism actually is.

158
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:15,440
The most concrete is Michael Graziano's attention schema theory.

159
00:13:15,720 --> 00:13:18,460
The brain constantly controls its own attention,

160
00:13:18,840 --> 00:13:22,180
and good control requires a model of the thing being controlled,

161
00:13:22,500 --> 00:13:25,040
the good regulator idea from episode 4.

162
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:28,480
So the brain builds a simplified model of its attention,

163
00:13:28,760 --> 00:13:32,360
and that model depicts not neurons competing for signal strength,

164
00:13:32,580 --> 00:13:33,460
but something simpler,

165
00:13:33,460 --> 00:13:36,660
An inner essence that grasps things, an awareness.

166
00:13:37,080 --> 00:13:42,540
When you say you're aware of the cup, Graziano argues, you're reporting the contents of that schema.

167
00:13:42,880 --> 00:13:49,800
It's the closest thing illusionism has to a wiring diagram, though Graziano himself dislikes the word illusion.

168
00:13:50,380 --> 00:13:55,800
Attention is real, he says, and awareness is the brain's useful caricature of it.

169
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:59,480
In episode 11, we'll treat it as a scientific theory.

170
00:13:59,480 --> 00:14:09,140
In 2021, Andrew Wilterson and Graziano built a deep reinforcement learning agent that had to control a simulated spotlight of attention to catch a falling ball.

171
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:13,960
Given an attention schema, a model of its own attention, it learned the task.

172
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:17,300
With the schema disabled, its performance collapsed.

173
00:14:17,560 --> 00:14:20,260
That shows a functional reason to have such a model.

174
00:14:20,540 --> 00:14:26,280
It says nothing about whether the agent experiences anything, and Graziano does not claim it does.

175
00:14:26,820 --> 00:14:30,400
Thomas Metzinger's Being No One argues that there is no such thing as a self.

176
00:14:30,700 --> 00:14:35,180
What exists is a phenomenal self-model that the brain cannot recognize as a model.

177
00:14:35,580 --> 00:14:38,560
It's transparent, like a window you look through rather than at,

178
00:14:38,720 --> 00:14:42,000
so you experience yourself as a self rather than as a model of one.

179
00:14:42,460 --> 00:14:47,080
Douglas Hofstadter's Gertl, Escher, Baugh, and later I Am a Strange Loop,

180
00:14:47,260 --> 00:14:49,200
argue that a self is a strange loop,

181
00:14:49,360 --> 00:14:53,220
a pattern in a symbol-processing system that comes to represent itself.

182
00:14:53,660 --> 00:14:59,080
The psychologist Nicholas Humphrey is a fellow traveler rather than a card-carrying illusionist.

183
00:14:59,320 --> 00:15:05,960
In Soul Dust and In Sentience, from 2022, he describes phenomenal consciousness as a kind

184
00:15:05,960 --> 00:15:11,920
of magical show the brain stages for itself, one that may have evolved because it makes being alive

185
00:15:11,920 --> 00:15:17,020
feel worth the trouble. But in his reply to Frankish, he resisted the word illusion and

186
00:15:17,020 --> 00:15:23,120
called his view phenomenal surrealism. The show is constructed, he thinks, but it is not a mistake.

187
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:26,480
It's the brain's own artwork, and it matters to us.

188
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:30,520
And Jeffrey Hinton has offered a version aimed squarely at machines.

189
00:15:30,880 --> 00:15:35,400
In an interview with The Globe and Mail, he rejected the idea of an inner theater.

190
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:44,380
On his account, talk of subjective experience is a way of describing what your perceptual system is telling you when it is not working properly.

191
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:48,240
The state of the world that would have to hold for your perception to be right.

192
00:15:48,460 --> 00:15:52,340
Then he imagined a chatbot with a camera, trained to point at objects.

193
00:15:53,220 --> 00:15:57,660
prism in front of the lens, and it points to the wrong place. Tell it about the prism,

194
00:15:57,820 --> 00:16:02,380
and it explains that the object is really straight ahead, but it had the subjective

195
00:16:02,380 --> 00:16:08,460
experience of it being off to one side. Hinton's claim is that it uses the phrase exactly as we do,

196
00:16:08,600 --> 00:16:14,900
and so has subjective experience just as we do. That seems too quick. The chatbot learned that

197
00:16:14,900 --> 00:16:20,160
phrase from us. It did, and that is what the machine episodes will call the gaming problem.

198
00:16:20,660 --> 00:16:24,560
A system trained on our words can give our reports without our reasons.

199
00:16:25,220 --> 00:16:30,840
Blaise Aguera E. Arcus, whom we met in episode four, takes the same line as Graziano.

200
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:37,640
Attention is a real computation, he argues, so a model of it is no more an illusion than a chair is.

201
00:16:38,140 --> 00:16:42,080
Like Graziano, he accepts the mechanism and resists the name.

202
00:16:43,260 --> 00:16:44,660
Fiction got here early.

203
00:16:45,020 --> 00:16:49,580
Peter Watts' 2006 novel Blindsight, which drew openly on Metzinger,

204
00:16:50,160 --> 00:16:53,900
sends a crew of modified humans to meet aliens that are vastly capable,

205
00:16:54,200 --> 00:16:57,100
and, it seems, entirely non-conscious,

206
00:16:57,480 --> 00:17:02,360
and asks whether consciousness might be a costly side effect rather than the crown of intelligence.

207
00:17:02,840 --> 00:17:05,600
It's the doing versus feeling split as a thriller,

208
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:09,900
and Watts has made it free to read online under a Creative Commons license.

209
00:17:10,340 --> 00:17:12,900
Notice that the novel works under either reading.

210
00:17:13,100 --> 00:17:16,520
A realist reads the aliens as lacking experience.

211
00:17:16,520 --> 00:17:21,740
An illusionist reads them as lacking the self-model and therefore lacking the illusion.

212
00:17:22,300 --> 00:17:28,220
Episode 10 has the real-world counterpart, patients with blindsight, who respond to what

213
00:17:28,220 --> 00:17:29,500
they say they cannot see.

214
00:17:30,580 --> 00:17:32,940
Now the objections at full strength.

215
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:38,620
In 2018, on the New York Review of Books website, the philosopher Galen Strawson called the

216
00:17:38,620 --> 00:17:42,920
denial of consciousness the silliest claim ever made, aiming chiefly at Dennett.

217
00:17:43,300 --> 00:17:46,260
Dennett replied that Strawson had misdescribed his view.

218
00:17:46,520 --> 00:17:52,660
consciousness is real, he said. It just is not what Strawson takes it to be. Strawson's answer

219
00:17:52,660 --> 00:17:58,340
was that what illusionists deny, the qualitative character of experience, is exactly the thing we

220
00:17:58,340 --> 00:18:04,160
know for certain. That's the core objection, and it can be put in one line. An illusion is a way

221
00:18:04,160 --> 00:18:10,320
things seem, and a seeming is an experience, so illusionism presupposes the very thing it denies.

222
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:15,980
John Searle made the point that where consciousness is concerned, the appearance is the reality.

223
00:18:16,520 --> 00:18:19,320
You can be wrong about whether there's a tomato in front of you.

224
00:18:19,520 --> 00:18:22,360
You cannot be wrong that it seems to you that there is.

225
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:26,360
Frankish's reply is that a seeming can be a representational state,

226
00:18:26,600 --> 00:18:30,040
a kind of judgment-like state produced by introspective mechanisms,

227
00:18:30,340 --> 00:18:32,400
without any extra phenomenal property.

228
00:18:33,300 --> 00:18:35,860
Representations can misrepresent without themselves glowing.

229
00:18:36,200 --> 00:18:39,780
And when you ask who is fooled, the answer is the system itself.

230
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:45,080
It's reasoning, memory, and report mechanisms, with no little observer inside.

231
00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:51,800
rejoinder is that seeming to have such qualities already is having them. Let me put that in my

232
00:18:51,800 --> 00:18:58,360
language. Illusionism says the brain's self-model is lossy and confidently wrong about its own

233
00:18:58,360 --> 00:19:05,560
internals. That's not exotic. Every learned model of a complex system is lossy, and nothing forces

234
00:19:05,560 --> 00:19:11,760
a system's model of itself to be accurate. That's a good translation, and it's why many engineers

235
00:19:11,760 --> 00:19:17,940
find the view natural. But the philosopher Philip Goff presses a different point. Consciousness is

236
00:19:17,940 --> 00:19:23,400
the one thing each of us knows more directly than anything else. All the evidence for any scientific

237
00:19:23,400 --> 00:19:28,920
theory, including the theory that experience is an illusion, reaches us through experience.

238
00:19:29,440 --> 00:19:34,540
So illusionism carries a heavier burden of proof than any ordinary empirical theory.

239
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:39,980
It asks us to doubt the medium through which we know everything else. And there's the illusion

240
00:19:41,760 --> 00:19:47,060
not yet solved. Why would evolution build a self-model that represents our states as ineffable

241
00:19:47,060 --> 00:19:53,720
and intrinsic? Graziano says simplification serves control. Humphrey says the show makes life feel

242
00:19:53,720 --> 00:19:58,980
worth living. Dennett says the interface hides what you do not need to see. These are promising

243
00:19:58,980 --> 00:20:05,460
stories, not finished explanations. A view that asks this much is easy to dismiss from outside.

244
00:20:06,060 --> 00:20:09,620
The philosopher Joe Carl Smith's essay Grokking Illusionism

245
00:20:09,620 --> 00:20:13,520
is a sustained attempt to understand it from the inside instead,

246
00:20:13,960 --> 00:20:16,520
and that is the right spirit in which to judge it.

247
00:20:17,520 --> 00:20:20,440
Why does this view matter so much for this series?

248
00:20:20,660 --> 00:20:24,320
Because it changes the machine question more than any other position.

249
00:20:24,560 --> 00:20:28,900
If illusionism is right, then asking whether a machine is conscious

250
00:20:28,900 --> 00:20:32,380
becomes asking whether it has the relevant kind of self-model.

251
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:37,420
Introspective representations of its own states as having phenomenal qualities,

252
00:20:37,700 --> 00:20:41,440
produced for the right reasons, and driving what it says about itself.

253
00:20:41,660 --> 00:20:46,500
That's a question about mechanisms, and interpretability research can, in principle,

254
00:20:46,940 --> 00:20:51,680
look for internal states that track the system's own processing and feed its self-reports.

255
00:20:52,180 --> 00:20:55,760
Episode 12 covers early experiments of that kind.

256
00:20:56,040 --> 00:20:57,260
But notice the trap.

257
00:20:57,260 --> 00:21:07,220
A language model trained on human writing can produce flawless reports of ineffable inner experience without any such self-model, just by learning how we talk.

258
00:21:07,460 --> 00:21:12,220
So illusionism does not make the machine question easy. It makes it empirical.

259
00:21:12,680 --> 00:21:16,220
The test is not what the system says. It's why it says it.

260
00:21:16,480 --> 00:21:21,540
In 2023, Dennett's own warning about AI was not about machines that feel.

261
00:21:21,780 --> 00:21:26,160
It was about machines that pass for people, which he called counterfeit people.

262
00:21:30,760 --> 00:21:32,820
Score it for me. Where does it stand?

263
00:21:57,260 --> 00:22:02,900
proof since it asks us to doubt what seems most certain, and the illusion problem, which remains

264
00:22:02,900 --> 00:22:09,240
a program rather than a result. What illusionism establishes, I think, is that the hard problem's

265
00:22:09,240 --> 00:22:15,120
most powerful intuitions could be the outputs of a mechanism. It does not establish that they are.

266
00:22:15,440 --> 00:22:20,460
That's a real achievement, and it's why the meta-problem comes back in the final episode,

267
00:22:20,460 --> 00:22:25,640
as one of the things that could change minds. Next episode, we lay out the whole map,

268
00:22:27,260 --> 00:22:29,780
on the hard problem, what each one costs,

269
00:22:29,980 --> 00:22:32,700
and what each one says about octopuses and machines.

270
00:22:33,160 --> 00:22:36,700
And at the end, a short, clearly marked trip to the edges.

